{"id":"security-pod-security-standards","corpus":"k8s","title":"Pod Security Standards","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Pod Security Standards"},{"kind":"p","text":"A detailed look at the different policy levels defined in the Pod Security Standards."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Pod Security Standards define three different policies to broadly cover the security spectrum. These policies are cumulative and range from highly-permissive to highly-restrictive. This guide outlines the requirements of each policy."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Profile Details"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Privileged"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Privileged policy is purposely-open, and entirely unrestricted. This type of policy is typically aimed at system- and infrastructure-level workloads managed by privileged, trusted users."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Privileged policy is defined by an absence of restrictions. If you define a Pod where the Privileged security policy applies, the Pod you define is able to bypass typical container isolation mechanisms. For example, you can define a Pod that has access to the node's host network."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Baseline"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Baseline policy is aimed at ease of adoption for common containerized workloads while preventing known privilege escalations. This policy is targeted at application operators and developers of non-critical applications. The following listed controls should be enforced/disallowed:"},{"kind":"p","text":"In this table, wildcards (*) indicate all elements in a list. For example, spec.containers[*].securityContext refers to the Security Context object for all defined containers. If any of the listed containers fails to meet the requirements, the entire pod will fail validation."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Restricted"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Restricted policy is aimed at enforcing current Pod hardening best practices, at the expense of some compatibility. It is targeted at operators and developers of security-critical applications, as well as lower-trust users. The following listed controls should be enforced/disallowed:"},{"kind":"p","text":"In this table, wildcards (*) indicate all elements in a list. For example, spec.containers[*].securityContext refers to the Security Context object for all defined containers. If any of the listed containers fails to meet the requirements, the entire pod will fail validation."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Policy Instantiation"},{"kind":"p","text":"Decoupling policy definition from policy instantiation allows for a common understanding and consistent language of policies across clusters, independent of the underlying enforcement mechanism."},{"kind":"p","text":"As mechanisms mature, they will be defined below on a per-policy basis. The methods of enforcement of individual policies are not defined here."},{"kind":"p","text":"Pod Security Admission Controller"},{"kind":"li","text":"Privileged namespace"},{"kind":"li","text":"Baseline namespace"},{"kind":"li","text":"Restricted namespace"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Alternatives"},{"kind":"p","text":"Other alternatives for enforcing policies are being developed in the Kubernetes ecosystem, such as:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Kubewarden"},{"kind":"li","text":"Kyverno"},{"kind":"li","text":"OPA Gatekeeper"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Pod OS field"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes lets you use nodes that run either Linux or Windows. You can mix both kinds of node in one cluster. Windows in Kubernetes has some limitations and differentiators from Linux-based workloads. Specifically, many of the Pod securityContext fields have no effect on Windows."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubelets prior to v1.24 don't enforce the pod OS field, and if a cluster has nodes on versions earlier than v1.24 the Restricted policies should be pinned to a version prior to v1.25."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Restricted Pod Security Standard changes"},{"kind":"p","text":"Another important change, made in Kubernetes v1.25 is that the Restricted policy has been updated to use the pod.spec.os.name field. Based on the OS name, certain policies that are specific to a particular OS can be relaxed for the other OS."},{"kind":"h3","text":"OS-specific policy controls"},{"kind":"p","text":"Restrictions on the following controls are only required if .spec.os.name is not windows:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Privilege Escalation"},{"kind":"li","text":"Seccomp"},{"kind":"li","text":"Linux Capabilities"},{"kind":"h2","text":"User namespaces"},{"kind":"p","text":"User Namespaces are a Linux-only feature to run workloads with increased isolation. How they work together with Pod Security Standards is described in the documentation for Pods that use user namespaces."},{"kind":"h2","text":"FAQ"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Why isn't there a profile between Privileged and Baseline?"},{"kind":"p","text":"The three profiles defined here have a clear linear progression from most secure (Restricted) to least secure (Privileged), and cover a broad set of workloads. Privileges required above the Baseline policy are typically very application specific, so we do not offer a standard profile in this niche. This is not to say that the privileged profile should always be used in this case, but that policies in this space need to be defined on a case-by-case basis."},{"kind":"p","text":"SIG Auth may reconsider this position in the future, should a clear need for other profiles arise."},{"kind":"h3","text":"What's the difference between a security profile and a security context?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Security Contexts configure Pods and Containers at runtime. Security contexts are defined as part of the Pod and container specifications in the Pod manifest, and represent parameters to the container runtime."}],"lemmas":{"securities":["security","securities"],"stand